[H1] Self-monitoring of an isolated power station
[P1] On a difficult-to-reach plateau, a station feeds a dispensary a radio relay, weather instruments and safety lighting The micro-network includes solar panels, lithium batteries and a small diesel generator As the road can remain closed for several days, a controller measures voltage, current, temperature and vibrations every 200 milliseconds Detailed data remains in place, only a compressed summary is transmitted when the link is operational. Medical equipment and communication are a priority
[P2] On 18 October 2025, a dust storm reduced solar output by 34% in fifteen minutes. The temperature of the battery cabinet rose by 8 °C, while the inverter fan produced an abnormal vibration. Instead of shutting down the station, the system turned off the secondary heating, reduced some of the lights, and started the rescue team. He identified three possible causes of debris on the panels, a worn-out bearing and increasing resistance in a battery connector.
[P3] After the storm, a head-mounted camera read the plates and alarm codes, then a local processor compared them to the offline manual No sensitive images were sent to the cloud The software required that the entire tag remain within a safe margin It also controlled the overall sharpness and that of the upper part An audible command asked to back off if an edge was missing, and four stable images were needed The engineers then confirmed the proposed order to clean the panels, replace the bearing, and then tighten the connector. The intervention lasted six minutes without interrupting a critical service reliable automation must recognize uncertainty and leave the final decision to the operator
============================================================

