[P1] Self-monitoring of an isolated power station
[P2] On a difficult-to-reach plateau, a station feeds a dispensary, a radio relay for weather instruments and safety lighting The micro-network includes solar panels, lithium batteries and a small diesel generator As the road can remain closed for several days, a controller measures voltage, current temperature and vibrations every 200 milliseconds. Detailed data remains in place, only a compressed summary is transmitted when the link is operational Medical and communication equipment is a priority
[P3] On 18 October 2025, a dust storm reduced solar output by 34% in fifteen minutes The temperature of the battery cabinet rose by 8 °C, while the inverter fan produced an abnormal vibration Instead of shutting down the station, the system cut off the secondary heating, reduced some lighting and started the rescue group. He identified three possible causes of debris on the panels, a worn-out bearing and increasing resistance in a battery connector.
[P4] After the storm, a head-mounted camera read the plates and alarm codes, then a local processor compared them to the offline manual No sensitive images were sent to the cloud The software required that the entire tag remain within a safe margin, it also controlled the overall sharpness and that of the top part A voice command asked to back off if an edge was missing, and four stable images were needed The engineers then confirmed the order suggested cleaning the panels, replacing the bearing, and then tightening the connector. The intervention lasted six minutes without interrupting a critical service reliable automation must recognize uncertainty and leave the final decision to the operator
============================================================

