[H1] Self-monitoring of an isolated power station
[P1] On a hard-to-reach plateau, a power station feeds a medical clinic, a radio relay. Weather instruments and safety lighting The micro-network includes solar panels, lithium batteries and a small diesel generator As the road may remain closed for several days, a controller measures voltage, current, temperature and vibrations every 200 milliseconds Detailed data remains in place, only a compressed summary is transmitted when the link is operational Medical and communication equipment is a priority
[P2] On October 18, 2025, a dust storm reduced solar output by 34% in fifteen minutes The temperature of the battery cabinet rose by 8 °C, while the inverter fan produced an abnormal vibration Instead of shutting down the station, the system cut off the secondary heating, reduced some lighting and started the rescue group He identified three possible causes of debris on the panels, a worn bearing and increasing resistance in a battery connector
[P3] After the storm, a head-mounted camera read the license plates and alarm codes, then a local processor matched them to the offline manual. The software required that the entire label remain within a safe margin, it also controlled the overall sharpness and the sharpness of the upper part A sound command asked to back up if an edge was missing, and four stable images were needed The engineers then confirmed the proposed order: clean the panels, replace the bearing. Then tighten the connector. The operation lasted six minutes without interrupting a critical ward. Reliable automation must recognize uncertainty and leave the final decision to the operator
